    "result_text": "The full suite is green (the repo's sole test class, `AcceptanceTest`, passes). Here's what I implemented for the shares-household feature.\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a `POST /api/owners` request sets `sharesHousehold: true` and there's an existing owner with the same last name and address, the new owner is now allowed to join that household, and every member (existing + new) is stamped with the same stable `householdId`, which is returned on the owner.\n\n### Changes\n\n**Behavior (`CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`)** \u2014 rewrote the household step so it now:\n- Collects existing owners matching the request's canonical (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed) last name + address.\n- No match \u2192 plain unique owner, no `householdId`.\n- Match but no opt-in \u2192 still a `409` (unchanged rejection).\n- Match + `sharesHousehold: true` \u2192 derives a **stable** id from the canonical last name + address (`HH-` + SHA-256 prefix), stamps it onto the existing members, and publishes it. Because it's derived from the shared fields, every joiner independently computes the identical value.\n\n**New pipeline pieces:**\n- `AssignOwnerHousehold` \u2014 a step after `build` that stamps the published id onto the newly built owner (no-op when there's no household).\n- `HouseholdId` \u2014 a small record carrying the id between the two steps as a dedicated variable type (avoids type-collision with other variables).\n- Wired `assignHousehold` into `owners.POST.yml` between `build` and `assignCode`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n**Persistence & schema:**\n- Added `householdId` field (`household_id` column) to the `Owner` entity.\n- Added the column to all four `schema.sql` files (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) and appended the value to the 10 positional owner seed inserts in hsqldb `data.sql`.\n\n**API contract:**\n- Added `householdId` as a read-only property on the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`; MapStruct maps it onto `OwnerDto` by name, so it's returned by both `GET` and the create response.\n\nThe test creates `a` (no opt-in), then `b` and `c` sharing a's last name/address with `sharesHousehold: true`, and asserts `b` and `c` come back with the same non-blank `householdId` \u2014 which now holds.",
